itions to interpolate from.\n                            // Actually, we compute position at t_next based on start of segment.\n                            // Start of segment for shadow: cumulative_time[active_seg]\n                            // Length of segment: cumulative_time[active_seg+1] - cumulative_time[active_seg]\n                            // Fraction: (t_next - cumulative_time[active_seg]) / length\n                            \n                            // Pre-calculate differences:\n                            // For shadow:\n                            // seg_start_time = (shadow_active_seg == 0) ? 0 : shadow_cum_time[shadow_active_seg];\n                            // But our cumulative_time array contains the END time of the segment.\n                            // Wait, in previous logic: shadow_cum_time[i] is time at point i+1?\n                            // Let's check: \n                            // COMPUTE_SEGMENTS: shadow_cum_time[idx] = shadow_cum_time[idx-1] + length\n                            // So shadow_cum_time[k] is cumulative time AT point k (where point k is index k).\n                            // No, we store cumulative time at the *end* of segment i at index i.\n                            // So shadow_cum_time[0] is end of segment 0 (time to point 1).\n                            // shadow_cum_time[1] is end of segment 1 (time to point 2).\n                            \n                            // Let's fix the cumulative time storage in the previous step to be clearer.\n                            // Let's say shadow_cum_time[k] is the time AT point k.\n                            // shadow_cum_time[0] = 0 (at point 0).\n                            // shadow_cum_time[1] = length(0->1).\n                            // shadow_cum_time[2] = length(0->1) + length(1->2).\n                            \n                            // In 4'b1111 (fixing time):\n                            // if idx == 0: shadow_cum_time[0] <= 0.\n                            // else: shadow_cum_time[idx] <= shadow_cum_time[idx-1] + length.\n                            // This makes shadow_cum_time[i] = cumulative time at point i.\n                            // Indices of points: 0 to shadow_cnt_reg - 1.\n                            // Segments: i to i+1. Length is at shadow_len_sq[i].\n                            // Time start of segment i: shadow_cum_time[i].\n                            // Time end of segment i: shadow_cum_time[i+1].\n                            \n                            // So we need to know time at start of current segment.\n                            // If shadow_active_seg = S, start time = shadow_cum_time[S].\n                            // End time = shadow_cum_time[S+1].\n                            \n                            // Current position in Q16.16:\n                            // pos = start_pos + (t - start_time) * vector / length\n                            // We need to calculate pos at t_next.\n                            \n                            // Let's do interpolation calculation now.\n                            \n                            // Shadow interpolation:\n                            // vec_x = shadow_dx[shadow_active_seg] (Q8.8)\n                            // vec_x_q16 = vec_x << 8 (Q16.16)\n                            // len = sqrt(shadow_len_sq[shadow_active_seg]) approx? No, use linear relation.\n                            // time_span = shadow_cum_time[shadow_active_seg + 1] - shadow_cum_time[shadow_active_seg]\n                            // delta_t = t_next - shadow_cum_time[shadow_active_seg]\n                            // pos_x = shadow_x_reg[shadow_active_seg] + (vec_x * delta_t) / time_span\n                            \n                            // This requires division. Hardware division is expensive.\n                            // However, delta_t / time_span is a fraction.\n                            // And t_next is either end of this segment (delta_t = time_span -> 1.0) \n                            // or end of timeline (partial).\n                            // \n                            // Wait, if we step through *endpoints* of segments:\n                            // t_next is ALWAYS an endpoint of some segment (or end of timeline).\n                            // So delta_t is ALWAYS equal to the full segment length for the dog whose segment ended.\n                            // For the other dog, delta_t is a partial segment.\n                            \n                            // So we need to calculate partial interpolation for the non-firing dog.\n                            // Division is required. \n                            // To avoid division, we can approximate or use bit-shifting if power of 2.\n                            // Or we can check distance at the end of the segment, AND at the point where the *other* dog finishes its segment.\n                            \n                            // Since we are looking for minimum distance, the minimum might be inside a segment (where derivative is 0).\n                            // This requires solving quadratic equation.\n                            // Since this is an FPGA, let's use a simplified approach:\n                            // Check all segment endpoints (where timing events happen).\n                            // This might miss internal minima, but is hardware friendly.\n                            // Or, check 2 points per segment: start and end. \n                            // The problem says \"Find minimum distance across all segments\".\n                            // \"Simplified approach: Check all segment endpoints + sample mid-points\".\n                            \n                            // Let's implement endpoint checking.\n                            // We iterate through `idx` from 0 to `shadow_cnt_reg - 1` (points).\n                            // Calc