pproach without division.\n                            // Interval: T_start to T_end.\n                            // Sample T_start and T_end.\n                            // This covers endpoints.\n                            // \n                            // What if min distance is strictly inside?\n                            // \"Simplified approach: Check all segment endpoints + sample mid-points\".\n                            // \n                            // Let's implement checking:\n                            // 1. All waypoints (t=0, t=T1, t=T2...)\n                            //    This covers start/end of segments.\n                            // 2. Mid-points of every combined segment.\n                            //    Combined segments are defined by sorted time points of both dogs.\n                            //    \n                            // Let's implement the time stepping in a compact way.\n                            // \n                            // State FIND_MIN_DIST:\n                            // \n                            // We have a register `mode` to decide which dog to sample.\n                            // \n                            // Let's go with the \"Check endpoints\" only first. It's most efficient.\n                            // We iterate through the points of the *shorter* path in time.\n                            // \n                            // If shadow_faster:\n                            //   Iterate i = 0...shadow_cnt-1.\n                            //   Time T = shadow_cum_time[i].\n                            //   Shadow Pos = shadow point i.\n                            //   Lydia Pos = Interpolate lydia at T.\n                            //   \n                            //   Wait, we need to interpolate. \n                            //   \n                            //   OK, I will implement a small iterative divider.\n                            //   Divider inputs: Numerator (T - T0